WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.750 --> 00:00:05.240
These days, the idea of erasing something
like a film from existence is kind of ridiculous

00:00:05.240 --> 00:00:07.310
— nothing disappears completely.

00:00:07.310 --> 00:00:11.980
Still, that doesn't mean that studios haven't
occasionally done their best to bury a particularly

00:00:11.980 --> 00:00:13.110
embarrassing film.

00:00:13.110 --> 00:00:17.810
Here are some movies that studios tried, and
mostly failed, to erase from existence.

00:00:17.810 --> 00:00:19.920
Fantastic Four

00:00:19.920 --> 00:00:26.320
Famously filmed on a budget of around $1 million,
the 1994 Fantastic Four film was never officially

00:00:26.320 --> 00:00:30.539
released, being unexpectedly cancelled just
a few weeks before its official premier.

00:00:30.539 --> 00:00:34.860
Since then, however, low-quality bootleg copies
of the film have found their way onto both

00:00:34.860 --> 00:00:36.600
the internet and DVD.

00:00:36.600 --> 00:00:37.560
"Look at me!

00:00:39.540 --> 00:00:40.620
Ahhh!"

00:00:40.940 --> 00:00:45.379
According to legend, the film was so bad that
the then-executive at Marvel Entertainment

00:00:45.379 --> 00:00:49.430
Avi Arad bought the rights
to the film moments after seeing an advanced

00:00:49.430 --> 00:00:52.960
copy and ordered that it be cancelled to protect
the Marvel brand.

00:00:52.960 --> 00:00:56.901
Arad would later claim that he additionally
ordered every remaining copy of the film be

00:00:56.901 --> 00:00:57.901
destroyed.

00:00:57.901 --> 00:01:02.320
Well, the joke's on Marvel: the film Arad
thought was so bad that it had to be buried

00:01:02.320 --> 00:01:06.680
has a higher Rotten Tomatoes score than two
of the three Fantastic Four films that have

00:01:06.680 --> 00:01:08.100
been released since.

00:01:08.100 --> 00:01:10.560
"Look at it!

00:01:10.560 --> 00:01:12.200
See it!"

00:01:12.200 --> 00:01:14.420
The Big Boss - Mandarin cut

00:01:14.420 --> 00:01:19.560
Regarded as the film that made Bruce Lee famous,
at least in Asia, The Big Boss is your standard

00:01:19.560 --> 00:01:24.720
1970s Hong Kong action movie and features
all of the Bruce Lee staples fans would eventually

00:01:24.720 --> 00:01:26.560
come to expect in his movies.

00:01:26.560 --> 00:01:30.170
The only problem is, a chunk of the movie
is missing.

00:01:30.170 --> 00:01:34.189
Before it was released, the original cut of
The Big Boss was effectively destroyed by

00:01:34.189 --> 00:01:35.720
Hong Kong censors.

00:01:35.720 --> 00:01:40.330
Known as "The Mandarin Cut," this more complete
version of Bruce Lee's first major film was

00:01:40.330 --> 00:01:45.110
more violent, and countless scenes were altered
or completely cut from the movie.

00:01:45.110 --> 00:01:49.310
It's highly unlikely that Bruce Lee fans will
ever see a complete version of the film with

00:01:49.310 --> 00:01:54.100
all of the cut content put back in, largely
because nobody knows if it still exists.

00:01:54.100 --> 00:01:56.340
Citizen Kane - Colorized version

00:01:56.340 --> 00:02:00.450
There's little that can be said about Citizen
Kane that hasn't already been said, so let's

00:02:00.450 --> 00:02:04.880
just talk about that time in the late 1980s
when media mogul Ted Turner decided that it

00:02:04.880 --> 00:02:09.050
would be a good idea to try and "fix" Citizen
Kane by colorizing it.

00:02:09.050 --> 00:02:12.930
According to an interview with colorist William
Schaeffer, at least 10 minutes of Citizen

00:02:12.930 --> 00:02:15.630
Kane was colorized before the project was
put to a stop.

00:02:15.630 --> 00:02:19.790
A friend of Orson Welles later claimed that
one of the last things the director said was

00:02:19.790 --> 00:02:24.180
"Don't let Ted Turner deface my movie with
his crayons."

00:02:24.180 --> 00:02:28.120
Depending on who you ask, the colorized footage
was either shelved or erased.

00:02:28.120 --> 00:02:33.070
To date, only around a minute of the footage
has emerged, most recently in an obscure '90s

00:02:33.070 --> 00:02:35.360
BBC documentary about the film.

00:02:35.360 --> 00:02:37.989
The Star Wars Holiday Special

00:02:37.989 --> 00:02:42.510
Until the prequels came along, the Star Wars
Holiday Special was long considered the single

00:02:42.510 --> 00:02:45.269
worst piece of Star Wars content ever made.

00:02:45.269 --> 00:02:49.510
It aired only once, the special was quickly
swept under the rug by Lucasfilm.

00:02:49.510 --> 00:02:54.129
George Lucas was reportedly very disappointed
in the special, and he saw to it that it was

00:02:54.129 --> 00:02:58.520
effectively buried by having it left out of
"complete" edition box sets and forbidding

00:02:58.520 --> 00:03:00.180
TV stations from airing it.

00:03:00.180 --> 00:03:05.040
According to legend, only one person had an
official copy of the special: Carrie Fisher,

00:03:05.040 --> 00:03:08.879
who claimed she received one in trade for
recording the commentary of one of the Star

00:03:08.879 --> 00:03:10.440
Wars DVD box sets.

00:03:10.440 --> 00:03:11.909
Why did she want a copy?

00:03:11.909 --> 00:03:15.349
She said that she wanted something she could
play at parties when she wanted people to

00:03:15.349 --> 00:03:16.349
leave.

00:03:16.349 --> 00:03:19.069
"It is so bad, it's not good.

00:03:19.069 --> 00:03:21.760
It's painful, really.

00:03:21.760 --> 00:03:23.280
It's dental work."

00:03:23.280 --> 00:03:25.909
Superstar: The Karen Carpenter Story

00:03:25.909 --> 00:03:30.790
Released in 1987, Superstar: The Karen Carpenter
Story is a short film that chronicles the

00:03:30.790 --> 00:03:33.319
final years of singer Karen Carpenter's life.

00:03:33.319 --> 00:03:38.350
Only instead of using archive footage or actors,
the film was made almost entirely with Barbie

00:03:38.350 --> 00:03:39.350
dolls.

00:03:39.350 --> 00:03:44.080
"Karen and Richard Carpenter… just a couple
of kids next door."

00:03:44.080 --> 00:03:47.780
Although the film was intended to provide
a sympathetic overview of Carpenter's life

00:03:47.780 --> 00:03:51.510
and the struggles she faced, it was decidedly
less kind to her family.

00:03:51.510 --> 00:03:56.129
Her brother Richard sued director Todd Haynes
for unlicensed use of The Carpenters' music,

00:03:56.129 --> 00:03:59.629
and virtually all copies of the film were
quietly destroyed.

00:03:59.629 --> 00:04:04.120
Haynes himself was banned from ever airing
the personal copy he had tucked away.

00:04:04.120 --> 00:04:08.560
This didn't stop resourceful fans — today,
several low quality versions have surfaced

00:04:08.560 --> 00:04:10.470
on YouTube.

00:04:10.470 --> 00:04:12.110
Nosferatu

00:04:12.110 --> 00:04:16.140
Considered by film buffs to be one of the
most influential horror movies ever made,

00:04:16.140 --> 00:04:21.370
Nosferatu was among the first films to adapt
Bram Stoker's Dracula to the big screen.

00:04:21.370 --> 00:04:25.610
The problem was, Stoker's widow never gave
anyone permission to make the movie.

00:04:25.610 --> 00:04:30.060
The first thing she did after the film came
out was sue Prana Film, the studio that released

00:04:30.060 --> 00:04:31.060
it.

00:04:31.060 --> 00:04:34.790
When the courts ruled that the film was a
blatant rip-off of Stoker's novel, they ordered

00:04:34.790 --> 00:04:39.690
that every copy be destroyed — an order
that was carried out so thoroughly, it's believed

00:04:39.690 --> 00:04:42.560
only a single copy of the film survived the
purge.

00:04:42.560 --> 00:04:46.430
This copy then circulated over the coming
decades until the copyright on the Dracula

00:04:46.430 --> 00:04:51.160
novel expired and the film could come back
to life.

00:04:54.940 --> 00:04:56.980
"Nosferatu!"

00:04:58.720 --> 00:04:59.720
Thanks for watching!

00:04:59.720 --> 00:05:02.050
Click the Looper icon to subscribe to our
